# ADR-0004 — Off-Device Transcription

Status: Accepted
Date: 2026-06-05

## Context

The device Sapiens Note emulates is a small e-ink MCU (Waveshare ESP32-S3). Modern
speech-recognition models (the Whisper family) need far more compute and memory
than such an MCU can provide, so transcription cannot run on the device itself.
This is the same conclusion the reference project (Pala Note) reached.

## Decision

**Capture is on-device; transcription is off-device.** A recording is stored
locally as 16 kHz mono WAV (Whisper's native rate, so no resample is needed), and
the **local web dashboard** (`sapiens-web`) transcribes it on demand through a
pluggable engine — whisper.cpp locally or the OpenAI API in the cloud
([ADR-0003](ADR-0003%20%E2%80%94%20Pluggable%20Transcription%20Engine)).

The capture loop is fully offline ([ADR-0001](ADR-0001%20%E2%80%94%20Offline%20First));
only the text output needs the engine.

## Consequences

Positive:
- the recorder stays cheap, low-power, and simple
- transcription quality is whatever engine you point it at (local or cloud)

Negative:
- a recording shows no transcript until you run it through the dashboard — the UI
  shows a clear "pending" state until then
- the local (whisper.cpp) path needs a downloaded model; the cloud path needs a key
